//? Title: Beta-VAE: Explicit Gaussian Noise and a Common Validation Criterion
//? Subtitle: Matched beta1/beta4 models, summed Bernoulli NLL and heldout factor probes
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A variational autoencoder maps images to uncertain latent codes and decodes their samples into
pixel probabilities. Balancing reconstruction against the prior changes both the learned codes
and the decoded images. This report derives the diagonal-Gaussian pathwise estimator and runs
matched beta1/beta4 models on294 unique binary circle/square images. Both use identical initial
weights, minibatch order and explicit noise banks; a common four-draw validation negative ELBO
selects each checkpoint. After only21 updates per model, beta4 has lower test KL but worse
reconstruction; decode(mu) MSE is0.07514/0.09722 versus0.04855 for the fitting-only mean.
Unfiltered examples, prior probabilities, latent traversals and fitting-only factor probes expose
these limitations. Higher beta alone does not establish disentanglement.
)
\toc

> A Gaussian posterior and a Bernoulli decoder

[Kingma and Welling's Auto-Encoding Variational Bayes](https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6114)
turns latent sampling into a differentiable transformation of explicit noise. For image x,
the encoder predicts mu(x) and log variance l(x), with
q(z|x)=N(mu,diag(exp(l))). Supply epsilon~N(0,I), independent of parameters, and set
z=mu+exp(l/2)⊙epsilon. The decoder produces logits a(z), then sigmoid probabilities p.
The reconstructed probabilities are neither sampled binary pixels nor the marginal likelihood.

[Higgins et al.'s beta-VAE](https://openreview.net/forum?id=Sy2fzU9gl) weights the KL penalty.
This small teaching architecture uses784→32ReLU→two4-coordinate linear heads and
4→32ReLU→784linear logits, with ordinary Glorot-uniform weights and zero biases.
For one explicit draw per image its objective is

$$
R_b=\sum_{j=1}^{784}[\operatorname{softplus}(a_{bj})-x_{bj}a_{bj}],\qquad
K_b=\frac12\sum_{d=1}^4[\mu_{bd}^2+e^{l_{bd}}-1-l_{bd}],\\
L_\beta=\frac1B\sum_b(R_b+\beta K_b).
$$
\alttext(Pixel reconstruction NLL sums784 features and Gaussian KL sums four latent coordinates; only images are averaged.)

Jensen's inequality gives log p(x)≥E_q[log p(x|z)]−KL(q||p), so beta1 estimates the
negative ELBO. For beta>1, L_beta=negativeELBO+(beta−1)KL in expectation: it penalizes
information in the posterior more strongly, without promising useful or axis-aligned factors.
No capacity annealing is used. All reductions matter: averaging pixels but summing latent
coordinates would change the effective regularization by784. Decode(mu) is a deterministic
control and generally differs from E_q[decode(z)] because the decoder is nonlinear.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/beta_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram          d(55, 1150);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle loss_style;
  loss_style.width  = 4.4;
